  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Les capacit&#xe9;s institutionnelles des organisations de travailleurs sont renforc&#xe9;es</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Generally, Cameroonian trade unions have been fragmented, with small membership bases that have limited their influence on policy and developmental issues affecting workers. To counter this, efforts made in the 2022-2023 biennium aimed at unifying these unions, leading to the creation of the Cameroon Workers&#x2019; Forum (CAWOF). CAWOF has provided a unified voice for workers, resulting in important achievements, such as the revision of the national minimum wage in 2023 and progress toward a fairer pension system.
The programme is designed to strengthen trade unions in Cameroon by enhancing their ability to address inequalities and influence policymaking through effective participation in social dialogue. A primary focus is placed on increasing the participation of women in trade unions and in social dialogue structures.
Despite the growing number of women in the workforce, their representation in leadership positions within trade unions and their participation in social dialogue structures remains low. To address this, the R&#xe9;seau Syndical pour la Promotion du Leadership F&#xe9;minin au Cameroun (RESYPLEF) was established in 2022. This network is aimed at encouraging female activism and improving women&apos;s involvement in decision-making processes at all levels of the labour movement.
Looking ahead to the 2024-2025 biennium, the programme will continue to focus on tackling inequalities, particularly in relation to gender, within both the unions and the broader social dialogue institutions. Capacity-building activities will be provided to support unions in addressing gender equality, pension reform, and workplace inequalities.
Women unionists will receive leadership training to enhance their participation in workplace negotiations and social dialogue. Workers in the metal waste collection sector in Yaound&#xe9; and Douala will also benefit from training to improve their conditions of work. The programme is implemented in collaboration with trade unions, and supported by capacity-bu</narrative>
  </description>
